DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ppttrraaccee()  system  call  provides a means by which one process (the
       "tracer") may observe and control the execution of another process (the
       "tracee"), and examine and change the tracee’s  memory  and  registers.
       It  is primarily used to implement breakpoint debugging and system call
       tracing.

       A tracee first needs to be attached to the tracer.  Attachment and sub‐
       sequent commands are per thread:  in  a  multithreaded  process,  every
       thread  can  be  individually  attached  to  a  (potentially different)
       tracer, or  left  not  attached  and  thus  not  debugged.   Therefore,
       "tracee" always means "(one) thread", never "a (possibly multithreaded)
       process".  Ptrace commands are always sent to a specific tracee using a
       call of the form

           ptrace(PTRACE_foo, pid, ...)

       where _p_i_d is the thread ID of the corresponding Linux thread.

       (Note that in this page, a "multithreaded process" means a thread group
       consisting of threads created using the cclloonnee(2) CCLLOONNEE__TTHHRREEAADD flag.)

       A  process  can  initiate a trace by calling ffoorrkk(2) and having the re‐
       sulting child do a  PPTTRRAACCEE__TTRRAACCEEMMEE,  followed  (typically)  by  an  eexx‐‐
       eeccvvee(2).   Alternatively,  one  process  may  commence  tracing another
       process using PPTTRRAACCEE__AATTTTAACCHH or PPTTRRAACCEE__SSEEIIZZEE.

       While being traced, the tracee will stop each time a signal  is  deliv‐
       ered,  even  if the signal is being ignored.  (An exception is SSIIGGKKIILLLL,
       which has its usual effect.)  The tracer will be notified at  its  next
       call  to  wwaaiittppiidd(2)  (or one of the related "wait" system calls); that
       call will return a _s_t_a_t_u_s value containing information  that  indicates
       the  cause of the stop in the tracee.  While the tracee is stopped, the
       tracer can use various ptrace operations  to  inspect  and  modify  the
       tracee.   The tracer then causes the tracee to continue, optionally ig‐
       noring the delivered signal (or even delivering a different signal  in‐
       stead).

       If the PPTTRRAACCEE__OO__TTRRAACCEEEEXXEECC option is not in effect, all successful calls
       to  eexxeeccvvee(2)  by the traced process will cause it to be sent a SSIIGGTTRRAAPP
       signal, giving the parent a chance to gain control before the new  pro‐
       gram begins execution.

       When  the  tracer  is finished tracing, it can cause the tracee to con‐
       tinue executing in a normal, untraced mode via PPTTRRAACCEE__DDEETTAACCHH.

