       When  a (possibly multithreaded) process receives a killing signal (one
       whose disposition is set to SSIIGG__DDFFLL and whose default action is to kill
       the process), all threads exit.  Tracees report their  death  to  their
       tracer(s).  Notification of this event is delivered via wwaaiittppiidd(2).

       Note  that the killing signal will first cause signal‐delivery‐stop (on
       one tracee only), and only after it is injected by the tracer (or after
       it was dispatched to a thread which isn’t traced), will death from  the
       signal happen on _a_l_l tracees within a multithreaded process.  (The term
       "signal‐delivery‐stop" is explained below.)

       SSIIGGKKIILLLL does not generate signal‐delivery‐stop and therefore the tracer
       can’t  suppress  it.   SSIIGGKKIILLLL kills even within system calls (syscall‐
       exit‐stop is not generated prior to death by SSIIGGKKIILLLL).  The net  effect
