BBUUGGSS
       On  hosts  with Linux 2.6 kernel headers, PPTTRRAACCEE__SSEETTOOPPTTIIOONNSS is declared
       with a different value than the one for Linux 2.4.  This leads  to  ap‐
       plications  compiled  with Linux 2.6 kernel headers failing when run on
       Linux 2.4.  This can be worked around by  redefining  PPTTRRAACCEE__SSEETTOOPPTTIIOONNSS
       to PPTTRRAACCEE__OOLLDDSSEETTOOPPTTIIOONNSS, if that is defined.

       Group‐stop  notifications  are sent to the tracer, but not to real par‐
       ent.  Last confirmed on 2.6.38.6.

       If a thread group leader is traced and exits  by  calling  __eexxiitt(2),  a
       PPTTRRAACCEE__EEVVEENNTT__EEXXIITT  stop will happen for it (if requested), but the sub‐
       sequent WWIIFFEEXXIITTEEDD notification will not be delivered  until  all  other
       threads  exit.   As  explained above, if one of other threads calls eexx‐‐
       eeccvvee(2), the death of the thread group leader will _n_e_v_e_r  be  reported.
       If  the  execed  thread  is  not traced by this tracer, the tracer will
       never know that eexxeeccvvee(2) happened.   One  possible  workaround  is  to
       PPTTRRAACCEE__DDEETTAACCHH  the thread group leader instead of restarting it in this
       case.  Last confirmed on 2.6.38.6.

       A SSIIGGKKIILLLL signal may still cause a PPTTRRAACCEE__EEVVEENNTT__EEXXIITT stop before actual
       signal death.  This may be changed in the future; SSIIGGKKIILLLL is  meant  to
       always  immediately  kill  tasks  even under ptrace.  Last confirmed on
       Linux 3.13.

       Some system calls return with EEIINNTTRR if a signal was sent to  a  tracee,
       but delivery was suppressed by the tracer.  (This is very typical oper‐
       ation: it is usually done by debuggers on every attach, in order to not
       introduce  a  bogus  SSIIGGSSTTOOPP).  As of Linux 3.2.9, the following system
       calls are affected (this list is likely incomplete): eeppoollll__wwaaiitt(2), and
       rreeaadd(2) from an iinnoottiiffyy(7) file descriptor.  The usual symptom of  this
       bug is that when you attach to a quiescent process with the command

           strace -p <process-ID>

       then, instead of the usual and expected one‐line output such as

           restart_syscall(<... resuming interrupted call ...>_

       or

           select(6, [5], NULL, [5], NULL_

       (’_’ denotes the cursor position), you observe more than one line.  For
       example:

               clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, {15370, 690928118}) = 0
               epoll_wait(4,_

       What   is  not  visible  here  is  that  the  process  was  blocked  in
       eeppoollll__wwaaiitt(2) before ssttrraaccee(1) has attached to  it.   Attaching  caused
       eeppoollll__wwaaiitt(2)  to  return  to user space with the error EEIINNTTRR.  In this
       particular case, the program reacted to EEIINNTTRR by checking  the  current
       time,  and  then executing eeppoollll__wwaaiitt(2) again.  (Programs which do not
       expect such "stray" EEIINNTTRR errors may behave in an unintended  way  upon
       an ssttrraaccee(1) attach.)

